= Quotes
*Passage 1 (pg. 686)*
#v(-1.5em)
#quote()[
  When we humans think, we certainly do change our own mental rules, and we change the rules that change the rules, and on and on---but these are, so to speak, "software rules"...
  Software rules on various levels can change; hardware rules cannot---in fact, to their rigidity is due the software's flexibility.
]
#v(-0.8em)
This is the major idea between the strange loops.
You have some collection of things which all influence each other but there is always an inviolate level whose rigidity provides the necessary conditions for the strange loop to exist.

*Passage 2 (pg. 695)*
#v(-1.5em)
#quote()[

  ... truth is too elusive for any human or any collection of humans ever to attain fully; and Artificial Intelligence, when it reaches the level of human intelligence---or even if it surpasses it---will still be plagued by the problems of art, beauty, and simplicity, and will run up against these things constantly in its own search for knowledge and understanding.
]
#v(-0.8em)
I thought this was interesting philosophically.
Truth is too broad of a concept for us and even a super intelligent AI to graph.
The hallmarks of intelligence will not be a solved problem once ASI is achieved.

= Key Terms / Mappings
- *Strange Loop or Tangled Hierarchy:* A system which involves multiple levels and layers acting on each other always supported by an inviolate layer.
- *Transcendentalism:*  19th-century creative movement emphasizing spiritual intuition, the innate goodness of humanity, and a deep connection to nature. Referred to as just "ism" by Hofstadter.
- *Shepard Tone:* An auditory illusion, named after cognitive scientist Roger Shepard, that creates the sensation of a sound continuously rising or falling in pitch without ever getting higher or lower.

= Counter Case or Limitation
I would have appreciated a callback to the Ant Fugue and ant colony analogy.
The tangled hierarchy of ants creates the illusion of a conscious ant colony making large scale decisions.

= Study Card Sentence
Self-reference creates consciousness, meaning, limits, and strange loops.

= Muddiest Point
What did Hofstadter mean by "every undecidable proposition is actually a Gödel sentence, asserting its own nontheoremhood in some system via code." (pg. 708)
This seems profound but also schizophrenic at the same time.
